  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 145 AM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe southern CA trough will merge with the upper low over \nsouthern NV on Tuesday. The winds over NM will stay fairly brisk\nout of the southwest with 700 mb speeds reducing to a 15 to 30 kt\nrange while the lee-side surface low sags into southeastern CO in\na weakened fashion. A more substantial and broadened dry slot will\nprecede the upper low, overtaking most of NM through Tuesday,\nshrinking rainfall opportunities to just scant showers over\nnorthwestern zones and perhaps along the eastern NM border with\nwest TX. Lowering heights would reduce temperatures across the\nforecast area Tuesday with eastern zones still remaining 5 to 10\ndegrees above climatology.\n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, the primary core of the polar jet will\nbe well northward into Canada with the subtropical fetch\nstretching northeastward across NM into the Great Lakes. The\norientation and placement of the jet streak incoming off of the \nPacific into British Columbia and Alberta will have significant \nramifications on the fate of the ConUS upper low Wednesday. The \nGFS and ECMWF have retained some run-to-run continuity to their \nrespective previous solutions from the past couple of days, but \nthese solutions vary drastically from model to model. The GFS/GEFS\nsolutions are mostly in favor of a northwest-to-southeast \noriented jet streak strengthening over British Columbia and \nAlberta Wednesday into Thursday, scooping up the low out and\nkicking it out east northeastward. Meanwhile ECMWF/EPS solutions \nare more prone to keep the low cut off from the polar jet, causing\nit to retrograde back into the southwestern US. Either outcome \nwould lead to cooler temperatures, but the former GFS/GEFS would \nlend to more tranquil and dry conditions while the latter \nECMWF/EPS scenarios are much different and wetter with the upper \nlow west of NM allowing remnant moisture from hurricane Simon to \nretrograde back westward into the state by the end of the work \nweek. Needless to say, large uncertainties remain in the extended\nforecast Wednesday onward. Ensemble means for PWATs are rather \ndry (0.3 to 0.5 inch over most of NM) which would suggest the \nlatter and wet solutions of the operational ECMWF might be \noverdone or outliers. For now, 10 to 30% POPs are in the forecast \nfor eastern zones for Thursday and Friday, reducing to 10 to 20% \nover central zones into Saturday.",
